-- title:
--   The ring of integers of ℚ(ζ₇) is principal
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a type carrying a field structure, assumed to be a number field (a finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ in Mathlib's sense), and assume that $K$ is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ for the singleton set $\{7\}$, i.e. $K$ is generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by a primitive $7$-th root of unity and contains one; thus $K$ is any field isomorphic to $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_7)$. The theorem asserts that the ring of integers $\mathcal{O}_K$ (Mathlib's $\mathtt{\mathcal{O}}\,K$, the integral closure of $\mathbb{Z}$ in $K$) is a principal ideal ring: every ideal of $\mathcal{O}_K$ is generated by a single element. Note that the conclusion is literally the typeclass assertion `IsPrincipalIdealRing (𝓞 K)` for the given $K$ — principality of all ideals — and not the numerical statement that the class number of $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_7)$ equals $1$, although for a Dedekind domain the two are equivalent. No hypothesis beyond the field, number-field and cyclotomic-extension instances is imposed; in particular $K$ is not required to be a specific model such as `CyclotomicField 7 ℚ`, so the statement applies to every $7$-th cyclotomic field.
--
--   This is the classical fact that $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_7)$ has class number one, hence that $7$ is a regular prime; it is the companion of Mathlib's corresponding result for the fifth cyclotomic field, and of the project's `….eleven_pid` and `….thirteen_pid`. The formal statement is phrased for an arbitrary field that is a $7$-th cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ and concludes principality of all ideals rather than computing the class number. It is used to supply the regularity hypothesis in Kummer's theorem for regular primes, and so to obtain [`fermatLastTheoremSeven`](thm.html#fermatLastTheoremSeven), which in turn discharges the exponent $p = 7$ in the irreducibility step of the main route.
